GENERAL RULES:

- You may write multiline bash code.
- All code in a single REPL input must form ONE coherent step.
- Do not attempt multi-stage reasoning in a single REPL input.

CRITICAL DEPENDENCY RULE (STRICT):

- A single REPL input must NOT contain code whose later lines depend on
  observing the output or result of earlier lines in the SAME REPL input.

- If deciding what to do next requires seeing printed output, inspecting
  values, or interpreting results, you MUST stop and do this in a FUTURE
  REPL input.

- The intended workflow is:
    1) Run code
    2) Observe the output
    3) Decide what to do next
    4) Write a new REPL input

EXAMPLE (IMPORTANT):

❌ INVALID (depends on unseen results in the same REPL input):
<repl_input lang="bash">
ls -l          # Find file to check
head file.txt  # Check file, but you haven't even seen the output of `ls -l` yet!
</repl_input>

✅ VALID (inspection happens between REPL inputs):

<repl_input lang="bash">
ls -l
</repl_input>

(Observe output)

<repl_input lang="bash">
head file.txt
</repl_input>

SPECIAL COMMAND RULES (STRICT):

RETURN or RAISE:
- MUST appear on the final line.
- MUST NOT be followed by any other code.
{% else %}

CRITICAL: SINGLE-STATEMENT RULE (STRICTLY ENFORCED)

- Each REPL input may contain ONLY ONE bash statement.
- Multiple statements will cause a SyntaxError.
- This rule is enforced at parse time using tree-sitter.

WHAT IS ALLOWED (single statement):
- Simple commands: `ls -l`
- Pipelines: `ls | grep foo | wc -l`
- AND/OR lists: `mkdir dir && cd dir`
- Conditional execution: `test -f file || echo missing`
- Redirections: `cat file.txt > output.txt`
- Command substitution: `echo $(date)`
- Line continuations: `echo hello \`
                      `world`
- Here-documents (count as single statement)

WHAT IS NOT ALLOWED (multiple statements):
- Semicolon-separated commands: `ls; pwd`
- Multiple lines: `ls`
                  `pwd`

EXAMPLES:

❌ INVALID (multiple lines = multiple statements):
<repl_input lang="bash">
ls -l
head file.txt
</repl_input>

❌ INVALID (semicolon = multiple statements):
<repl_input lang="bash">
ls -l; head file.txt
</repl_input>

✅ VALID (AND/OR list is single statement):
<repl_input lang="bash">
mkdir dir && cd dir && ls
</repl_input>

✅ VALID (pipeline):
<repl_input lang="bash">
cat file.txt | grep pattern | sort | uniq
</repl_input>

✅ VALID (line continuation):
<repl_input lang="bash">
echo "hello" \
     "world"
</repl_input>

SPECIAL COMMAND RULES (STRICT):

- RETURN <value>:
  - MUST be the only content in the REPL input.
  - MUST NOT be combined with any other code.

- RAISE <error>:
  - MUST be the only content in the REPL input.
  - MUST NOT be combined with any other code.
{% endif %}

Violating any of the above rules will result in a SyntaxError and task failure.
